      "id": 1,
      "text": "Empire’s original name, McCoy’s Station, came from a railroad stop that once served the village.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.villageofempire.org/about",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before settling on Empire, the hamlet was called Stumptown because early settlers felled so many maple trees that stumps littered the streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.villageofempire.org/about",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The quirky nickname Shanghai stuck when a settler introduced Shanghai chickens to the area, giving the village a touch of rural charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.villageofempire.org/about",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A post office has been operating in Empire since 1886, long before the village was officially incorporated.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empire%2C_Ohio",
